\end{align*}
We can solve this system of equations by elimination. Multiplying the first equation by 2 and the second equation by 3, we get
\begin{align*}
20t&=6s+3g\
2t+3g&=3s\
\end{align*}
Subtracting the second equation from the first, we get
\begin{align*}
17t&=3g\
\end{align*}
Dividing both sides by 3, we get
\begin{align*}
\frac{17}{3}t&=g\
\end{align*}
Substituting this into the second equation, we get
\begin{align*}
2t+\frac{17}{3}t&=3s\
\end{align*}
Multiplying both sides by 3, we get
\begin{align*}
6t+17t&=9s\
\end{align*}
Combining like terms, we get
\begin{align*}
33t&=9s\
\end{align*}
Dividing both sides by 9, we get
\begin{align*}
\frac{33}{9}t&=s\
\end{align*}
Simplifying, we get
\begin{align*}
\frac{11}{3}t&=s\
\end{align*}
Substituting this into the first equation, we get
\begin{align*}
10t&=3\left(\frac{11}{3}t\right)+g\
\end{align*}
Simplifying, we get
\begin{align*}
10t&=11t+g\
\end{align*}
Subtracting $11t$ from both sides, we get
\begin{align*}
-t&=g\
\end{align*}
Substituting this into the second equation, we get
\begin{align*}
2t+\frac{17}{3}t&=3\left(\frac{11}{3}t\right)\
\end{align*}
Simplifying, we get
\begin{align*}
2t+\frac{17}{3}t&=11t\
\end{align*}
Subtracting $11t$ from both sides, we get
\begin{align*}
-\frac{19}{3}t&=0\
\end{align*}
Since $t$ is nonzero, we have $-\frac{19}{3}t=0$, which implies $t=0$. However, this contradicts the given information that $t$ is nonzero. Therefore, there is no solution to this system of equations.

